  The KA630 processor console is pretty much limited to E, D, B and a
  few other very primitive commands.

  To view the Ethernet address, assuming one DEQNA or DELQA network
  controller is present, you can use cryptic commands such as:

  >>> E/P/W/N:5 20001920

  There is no HELP command, and an error message of the order of "Eh?"
  would be considered verbose for this particular console program.

  If you have a bootable tape at its default CSR address configuration
  (more on CSRs via the information at the "serpentine" reference below)
  the usual is B MUA0 -- assuming a 9-track magtape or TK50, you can make
  a tape for this on another OpenVMS VAX system using STABACKIT.  Or you
  can find a distribution tape -- making a tape from a network or CD
  distro kit is possible, but it's a manual process and it's not feasible
  from a KA630 system at the >>> dead sargeant prompt.

  Easiest way to get this box booted under OpenVMS VAX is to either
  take a disk to another system, or try try a semi-suicidal Standalone
  BACKUP.  

  For the former, haul the RD disk over to another box running OpenVMS
  VAX, and use BACKUP/IMAGE to restore the VMSxxx.B saveset onto the
  disk, then MOUNT the resulting disk and copy the VMSxxx.% savesets
  onto the same or another disk.  Haul the disk back, and book it. 
  An RD-class drive hanging off a system with one MSCP disk controller
  -- an RQDX2 or (preferably) RQDX3 controller -- will usually be DUA0:,
  DUA1:, DUA2: or similar.  Once connected back to the KA630, B DUA0
  (no colon), or B DUA1, etc.

  For the latter case, get somebody to build a TK50 with Standalone 
  BACKUP using STABACKIT, then either load the same or the next tape
  with copies of VMS%%%.% savesets.  (Building a tape from disk savesets
  is a little trickier than it might sound.)  Boot the Standalone BACKUP
  tape with B MUA0 (again, no colon), and -- once Standalone BACKUP starts
  up -- issue the command BACKUP/IMAGE/REWIND MUA0:/SAVE DUA0:/VERIFY. 
  When that finishes, halt the system (see the FAQ for the console
  instructions, and for details on the BREAK/HALT operation at the
  console) and then B DUA0 (again, no colon) to start up the disk.

  VTstar is available on the OpenVMS Freeware V7.0 distro, and has a
  better VT terminal emulation -- the default Microsoft Windows terminal
  emulator is not particularly good at emulating a VT terminal, and 
  various of the OpenVMS terminal applications will assume VT sequences.

  As for pin-outs, the OpenVMS FAQ has pointers to various documents and
  URLs, and information on adapters.

  As for the controllers you have, you'll want to determine the controller
  name, either directly, or from the controller Mnnnn number usually found
  on or near the handle of the controller, or on the spine of the module.
  M7606 is the KA630 module, for instance, and M7609 is one of the more
  common MS630-series memory modules.  If you can't find the M-number 
  translation via Google (which would be unusual), there are folks
  around with lists.

  It will be easiest if you acquire a real OpenVMS VAX distribution kit
  for this box, too -- building your own distribution kit isn't intended
  nor documented as an entry-level project.   Since these projects are
  never done the easiest way, poke back through the newsgroup archives
  for previous discussions of how to create your own distro -- basically,
  for more details on what I've posted above.

  You will want to visit the Ask The Wizard area (see the FAQ) and look
  for the word "serpentine" there -- you will need to know how to
  configure the Q-bus, because it almost certainly doesn't work the
  way you or anyone else familiar with current I/O buses might expect.
  Existing topics in the Ask The Wizard area and here in the newsgroup
  archives (this group, or comp.os.vms) tend to use the word "serpentine"
  to describe how the bus is wired, and which slots can be used for what
  purpose within which of the various available Q-bus enclosures around.

  For platforms other than OpenVMS VAX on this MicroVAX II series KA630
  processor, you'll want to ask others for assistance.

