Matt Britt wrote:
> I recently got it in my head to improve some of the terribly written
> computer-related articles on Wikipedia (free, online, editable-by-anybody
> encyclopedia).  I've been trying to locate some figures indicating the
> power consumption of early DEC discrete component transistorized computers
> (like the PDP-8s and early 11s), but haven't found much of use.
> Hopefully some folks who hang around here remember the PDP-8 days and
> could help me out...  If anyone can point me in the right direction for
> locating these kinds of figures, it would be much appreciated!
>
> Thanks in advance!
>
> --
> Matt Britt
> CmpE Undergraduate
> Georgia Institute of Technology

DEC's "Small Computer Handbook", 1968, p 289, gives 780W for a
"standard PDP-8/I", and the 1971 edition p 11-6 gives 450W for a
PDP-8/E. Both books list consumption of peripherals like Teletype
(150W), DECtape (110W), card reader (60W), etc. separately.

The 1970 "PDP11 Handbook", p82, gives 400W for a PDP-11/20.

The various "Microcomputer Handbooks" for LSI-11 etc. just give
board-by-board power consumptions, so it's a  difficult to give typical
figures quickly. I'll try later (or just measure some) if no one else
comes up with anything.

A root around at http://www.bitsavers.org/ may find you more (note
their request to use mirrors rather than their own bandwidth).

Chris



