> comes up with anything.

> A root around at http://www.bitsavers.org/ may find you more (note
> their request to use mirrors rather than their own bandwidth).

For systems such as a Unibus or Q-Bus PDP-11, you really have to take a look
at what modules are installed, in order to know how much power the system
will draw.  For example, a BA123 has a 600W power supply (assuming my memory
is working), but the odds are you're only going to have a small portion of
that backplane populated, and if you've upgraded to modern disks, they'll
also draw less power than the old 5.25" full hight MFM drives.

Another example is the PDP-11/44, the manual says that it needs to be on a
15A circuit, in reality, you can get away with a smaller circuit, especially
if you don't have several big drives.  That 15 Amps is partially to handle
the 14" drives spinning up.

		Zane



