David J Dachtera wrote:
> Folks,
> 
> My little Alpha developed a problem: the fan in the power supply no
> longer spins freely. (AlphaStation 200 4/233)
> 
> Now - does anyone know where I can get a new fan for an Astec SA180-3505
> power supply? 


0. Unplug it
1. Take it apart.
2. Look at the markings on the fan to find out voltages,
currents, and the like. There are ALWAYS markings, although the
ones you want may be hidden under a sticker.
3. Find a replacement fan (it doesn't have to be a perfect match)
4. Install the replacement in (some soldering may be required).

Before replacing it see if there's a tiny hole for adding oil.  A few
fans are like that.  If you have one try a drop of oil and see if it
starts working again.

I've replaced fans this task on many power supplies and it's not 
terribly difficult to do.  Just be careful that you don't touch anything
other than the fan, as there can be charged capacitors inside the power
supply.  If you're really lucky the fan will use the same type of 
connector as is used to plug into PC motherboards, in which case you can 
easily get a fan with those connectors, and you won't even have to cut 
any wires.  If you're unlucky (about half the time) the connector will 
be something you've never seen before, can't find in any catalog, and 
have no hope whatsoever of obtaining.  For those there's nothing wrong 
with cutting the wires, slipping on some shrink tube, soldering the new 
wires on, and then pulling the shrink tube down and shrinking it to 
insulate.  I'm not a fan of either electrical tape or connector nuts for 
this application.

