In article <7p-dnfOsdoGeZ9_eRVn-rg@sunset.net>, Crabs <Crabs@ihatespam.com> writes:
:GM wrote:
:> Looking to upgrade to a PCI video Card
:> The Machine is a HP Alphaserver 800 5/33

:It depends entirely on what OS you're using and how you choose to boot 
:the system.
:SRM is *EXTREMELY* fussy about video cards whereas AlphaBios is not 
:quite so fussy.

  The host operating systems are usually the gating factor, not SRM.

  There is a substantial variation in the I/O interfaces for the
  various graphics controllers, and the lifetimes of these products
  can be less than the lifetime of the average fruitfly.  Generating
  and keeping drivers for graphics controllers remains "interesting"
  due to the product churn, and particularly also given that various
  of the controllers on the market even lack published I/O interface 
  specifications.


:If you plan to use SRM then you're limited to a small handful of cards. 
:Elsa Gloria Synergy (8mb), Diamond Fire Pro GL1000 (with Permedia 2 
:chipset), Powerstorm 4D10T, Powerstorm 3D30 or Powerstorm 4D20 are the 
:ones that come to mind.  There might be a few others.

  I would personally probably shop for a PowerStorm 3D30 or PowerStorm
  4D20 series -- these should work in most any of the operating systems
  you might choose.  The ELSA GLoria Synergy (largely equivalent to the 
  PowerStorm 4D10T series) will also work, though it tends to be a 
  low-end card, and rather slow for various operations.

  The PowerStorm 3D30 (PBXGB-AA) and PowerStorm 4D20 (PBXGB-CA) are
  (still) screamingly-fast 2D PCI graphics cards, FWIW.

  The current crop of graphics controllers generaly require EV6-class
  processors and flat I/O addressing, a requirement which your system
  and the other and older Alpha boxes do not offer.

