In article <howard-CF7803.21384003102005@news.supernews.com>, Howard S Shubs <howard@shubs.net> writes:
:In article <dhr5bf$8qk_004@s1263.apx1.sbo.ma.dialup.rcn.com>,
: jmfbahciv@aol.com wrote:
:
:I've got a machine that natively speaks DSSI and old SCSI, for which no 
:one makes devices these days.  I'm doing some forward thinking for when 
:I need to add a disk which the machine can't currrently connect to.

  Assuming OpenVMS, a cluster satellite boot off an OpenVMS host with
  slightly newer disks will allow a VAX 4000 model 500 to operate sans
  disks.  You can operate as a diskless satellite, or (better, in terms
  of performance) as a satellite with local page and swap.  Do consider
  adding (more) physical memory here, if you follow this approach.

  For local storage, the usual approach for DSSI is the HSD05 series
  DSSI to (old) SCSI -- with proper SCSI adapters, I've usually been
  able to get newer SCSI disks to work with older controllers, too.
  Or consider that Nemonix SCSI controller widget that was mentioned
  elsewhere.  Or swap out the old VAX hardware for a VAX emulator, or
  for native operations on a newer VAX, Alpha or Integrity platform.

  Usual approach for these old systems that absolutely must stay
  operational is to actually acquire or to contract for long-term
  availability of spare parts.

  I am aware of no Q-bus to USB, nor Q-bus to IEEE 1304/FireWire.
  These I/O technologies post-date the Q-bus by twenty years or so.
  The Q-bus configurations -- being among the oldest buses in some
  use -- are also among the most limited configurations still in use.

  The Q-bus specifications are available and are (or were) quite
  well understood, so it is conceivable that you could create an
  I/O card using, say, an FPGA, and "roll your own" I/O support.
  Or you could ask a vendor to create a custom Q-bus card for you.
  (I'd certainly tend to look to move to newer hardware and would
  expect this to be the lowest hardware cost, but there are probably
  cases around where fully-custom hardware might well be more
  "acceptable" than simply moving to newer hardware.)

